Dynamic Structural Plasticity for Artificial Intelligence

A Theoretical Framework for Self-Reorganizing Computational Systems

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

Contemporary artificial intelligence, most notably in the form of Large Language Models (LLMs), is characterized by its mastery of parameter plasticity within a static computational topology. This fundamental limitation means that while an LLM can learn new mappings, it cannot inherently alter the very structure of its computation. Biological intelligence, in stark contrast, is defined by a continuous, dynamic interplay between activity, structure, and plasticity.

This thesis proposes a unified theoretical framework—Dynamic Structural Plasticity for Artificial Intelligence (DSP-AI)—for creating artificial systems capable of self-reorganization. Moving beyond the static graph of a conventional network, we posit a system whose fundamental state is a tuple of computational topology, parameters, and meta-plasticity. The system operates on a principle of structure as a hypothesis, constantly proposing, testing, and pruning or consolidating new computational pathways.

The framework introduces a Structural Plasticity Engine (SPE) that governs this adaptive process through a mathematically defined objective function balancing performance, novelty, adaptability, and cost. A key contribution is the formalization of counterfactual structural evaluation as a mechanism for assessing the utility of a new connection or module. This framework is designed to be model-agnostic, applicable to both small, theoretically tractable networks and as an augmentation layer for large, pre-existing models like LLMs.

We argue that this approach moves the field from learning within a structure to learning how to reorganize the structure itself. The central hypothesis is that such a system will not only achieve superior continual adaptation but may also give rise to emergent computational capabilities that are difficult or impossible to specify a priori. This work provides a formal foundation for experimentally investigating the genesis of self-organized intelligence.

